what is form in react?
A form in react is used to collect the input data such as
username,password and vendor details.
In react we have event handlers such as onclick will handle the user clicking actions, onSubmit is used to handle the submit the form and 
onChange is used to capture the input data 
usually we will store the form value in react state

const [username, setUsername] = useState("");

<input
  value={username}
  onChange={(e) => setUsername(e.target.value)}
/>

what is controlled components?
the controlled components are input values these input values are controlled by react state.
the values are comes from state, whenever the user changes the input ,
we update the state using onChange.

What is an uncontrolled component?
An uncontrolled components is a react form inputs where react state does not control directly, Instead, the DOM manages the value, and we can access it using a ref.

import { useRef } from "react";

function Login() {
  const usernameRef = useRef();

  const handleSubmit = (e) => {
    e.preventDefault();

    console.log(usernameRef.current.value);
  };

  return (
    <form onSubmit={handleSubmit}>
      <input ref={usernameRef} />

      <button type="submit">
        Login
      </button>
    </form>
  );
}

Controlled vs uncontrolled
Controlled	                        Uncontrolled
React state manages value	  DOM manages value
Uses useState	              Usually uses useRef
value + onChange	              ref

Why do we use event.preventDefault()?

By default when an html form submitted,the browser may reload the page and
and may lead default submission of form, in react we usually want to submit by ourself, so we use event.preventDefault() to stop the default browser behavior.

const handleSubmit = (event) => {
  event.preventDefault();

  // Call API here
};


How do you handle multiple input fields?
For multiple fields, i will use one state management object and use common
onchange handler,i will use input name attribute which field is updated.
Example:

const [formData, setFormData] = useState({
  name: "",
  email: "",
  phone: ""
});

const handleChange = (event) => {
  const { name, value } = event.target;

  setFormData({
    ...formData,
    [name]: value
  });
};

Inputs:

<input
  name="name"
  value={formData.name}
  onChange={handleChange}
/>

<input
  name="email"
  value={formData.email}
  onChange={handleChange}
/>

<input
  name="phone"
  value={formData.phone}
  onChange={handleChange}
/>

The important part is:

[name]: value

If the user changes email:

name = "email"
value = "renuka@gmail.com"

        ↓
formData.email = "renuka@gmail.com"

How do you validate a form?
I validate a form submitting before to the backend, i can perform client side validation such as email field is required, password length,required fields
through backend also i will validate the input fields , by front-end alone is not required.

const handleSubmit = (event) => {
  event.preventDefault();

  if (!formData.name) {
    console.log("Name is required");
    return;
  }

  if (!formData.email) {
    console.log("Email is required");
    return;
  }

  // Call API
};


How do you handle checkbox and radio buttons?
for checkbox i will use checked instead of value

const [isActive, setIsActive] = useState(false);

<input
  type="checkbox"
  checked={isActive}
  onChange={(e) => setIsActive(e.target.checked)}
/>

e.target.checked gives:

true  → checked
false → unchecked

Radio buttons are normally used when the user should select one option from a group.
const [gender, setGender] = useState("");

<input
  type="radio"
  name="gender"
  value="male"
  checked={gender === "male"}
  onChange={(e) => setGender(e.target.value)}
/>

<input
  type="radio"
  name="gender"
  value="female"
  checked={gender === "female"}
  onChange={(e) => setGender(e.target.value)}
/>

How do you handle dropdown/select fields?
const [role, setRole] = useState("");

<select
  value={role}
  onChange={(e) => setRole(e.target.value)}
>
  <option value="">Select Role</option>
  <option value="vendor">Vendor</option>
  <option value="employee">Employee</option>
  <option value="admin">Admin</option>
</select>

For dropdowns, I use the select element and manage its selected value using state. The onChange event updates the state whenever the user selects another option.
